Definitions needed before using these terms include: the dimension/signature and
orientation of spacetime; spin structure and spinor bundles; the gauge principal
bundle and connection; each matter representation and field statistics; the
off-shell multiplet and auxiliary fields; the R-symmetry and its background
connection; the supercharge(s) and their reality/normalization convention; and
the background Killing-spinor equations. A rigid supersymmetric background is
not merely a curved manifold: it is a metric plus specified background
supergravity/R-symmetry/auxiliary fields admitting a nonzero solution of the
chosen fermion-variation equations. State the spin structure, reality contour,
R-charges and background fields, and explicitly compute the square `Q^2` as the
combination of isometry, gauge, R-symmetry and flavor transformations used in
the localization complex. Do not assume every metric admits such a background.

