        "statement": "Let $T^4=(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^4$ have global angular coordinates $x^1,\\ldots,x^4$ and orientation $\\omega=dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3\\wedge dx^4$. Use the graded algebra $\\Omega^\\bullet(T^4;\\mathbb C)$ and integrate an inhomogeneous form by integrating its degree-four component over $T^4$. Let $Q=d$ be the exterior derivative, determined by $df=\\sum_i(\\partial_i f)\\,dx^i$, $d(dx^i)=0$, and the graded Leibniz rule. Set $O=dx^1\\wedge dx^2$, $S=dx^3\\wedge dx^4$, and $V=\\sin(x^3)\\,dx^4$. For $t\\in\\mathbb R$, define $Z(t)=\\int_{T^4}\\bigl(Oe^{-S-tQV}\\bigr)_{[4]}$. The degree-four density is $-\\bigl(1+t\\cos(x^3)\\bigr)\\omega$, which depends on $t$ at points where $\\cos(x^3)\\ne0$, but $Z(t)=-(2\\pi)^4$ for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$.",
