@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+---
+id: def-equivariant-elliptic-complex-index
+kind: definition
+title: "Equivariant elliptic-complex index and character"
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+category: quantum-field-theory-mathematics
+page: qftx-equivariant-index
+dependency_level: 0
+deps: []
+dependency_roles: {}
+provenance:
+  statement: literature-derived
+  proof: not-applicable
+verification:
+  precheck: n/a
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "Zelin Yi, A Groupoid Proof of the Lefschetz fixed point formula"
+      url: "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2003.00955"
+    - title: "Nicole Berline, Ezra Getzler, and Michèle Vergne, Heat Kernels and Dirac Operators"
+      url: "https://djvu.online/file/ruwkno3rtNaI5"
+---
+
+## Definition
+
+Let $T$ be a compact torus and $M$ a compact smooth manifold without boundary. A smooth left $T$-action is a jointly smooth map $a:T\times M\to M$ with $a(e,x)=x$ and $a(st,x)=a(s,a(t,x))$. Let $E^0,\ldots,E^N$ be finite-rank complex vector bundles on $M$. For each $j<N$, let $d_j:C^\infty(M,E^j)\to C^\infty(M,E^{j+1})$ be a linear differential operator of finite order, and put $d_{-1}=d_N=0$. The sequence is a complex when $d_{j+1}d_j=0$ for every $j$. It is **elliptic** when, for every $x\in M$ and every nonzero real cotangent vector $\xi\in T_x^*M$, the sequence of principal-symbol maps
+
+$$0\longrightarrow E_x^0\xrightarrow{\sigma(d_0)(x,\xi)}E_x^1\xrightarrow{\sigma(d_1)(x,\xi)}\cdots\xrightarrow{\sigma(d_{N-1})(x,\xi)}E_x^N\longrightarrow0$$
+
+is exact. Suppose each bundle has a smooth $T$-linearization $\rho_t^j:E_x^j\to E_{t\cdot x}^j$, satisfying the group-action law, and the induced section maps
+
+$$ (U_t^j s)(x):=\rho_t^j\bigl(s(t^{-1}\cdot x)\bigr) $$
+
+commute with $d_j$. Require the cohomology spaces $H^j(E^\bullet):=\ker d_j/\operatorname{im}d_{j-1}$ to be finite-dimensional. The representation ring $R(T)$ is the Grothendieck ring of finite-dimensional continuous complex representations of $T$. Define
+
+$$\operatorname{Ind}_T(E^\bullet):=\sum_{j=0}^N(-1)^j[H^j(E^\bullet)]\in R(T),\qquad \chi_{E^\bullet}(t):=\sum_{j=0}^N(-1)^j\operatorname{tr}\!\left(U_t^j\bigm|_{H^j(E^\bullet)}\right).$$
+
+For a two-term complex $0\to C^\infty(M,E^+)\xrightarrow{D^+}C^\infty(M,E^-)\to0$, this gives $[\ker D^+]-[\operatorname{coker}D^+]$. This definition concerns elliptic complexes, not transversally elliptic operators.
+
+## Definition references
+
+[Zelin Yi, §§1–2: elliptic complexes, induced cohomology maps, and the Lefschetz character](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2003.00955)
+
+[Berline–Getzler–Vergne, Chapter 6 §6.1: the equivariant Dirac index as a virtual character in $R(G)$](https://djvu.online/file/ruwkno3rtNaI5)
+
+## Remarks
+
+For a $T$-invariant operator on a $T$-manifold, **transversally elliptic** means its principal symbol is invertible away from the zero section after restriction to $T_T^*M:=\{(x,\xi):\xi(X_M(x))=0\text{ for every }X\in\mathfrak t\}$, where $X_M$ is the vector field generated by $X$. A transversally elliptic operator can have an infinite-dimensional kernel; for a compact torus its irreducible characters can occur with finite multiplicity but infinitely many weights, so its index is generally a distribution on $T$, not an element of $R(T)$. It is therefore excluded from this definition.
