+---
+id: lem-equivariant-relative-tangent-groupoid-heat-trace-limit
+kind: lemma
+title: "Relative tangent-groupoid heat-trace limit at simple fixed points"
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+category: quantum-field-theory-mathematics
+page: qftx-equivariant-index
+dependency_level: 0
+deps: []
+dependency_roles: {}
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: literature-derived
+  proof: ai-altered
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "Zelin Yi, A Groupoid Proof of the Lefschetz fixed point formula"
+      url: "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2003.00955"
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Let $T$ be a compact torus acting smoothly by isometries on a compact Riemannian manifold $M$ without boundary. Let $E^0,\ldots,E^N$ be a finite-rank Hermitian bundle complex, with first-order differential maps $d_j$ satisfying $d_{j+1}d_j=0$, and assume its principal-symbol sequence is exact for every nonzero real cotangent vector. Suppose the $T$-linearizations are unitary and commute with the differentials. Put $E^{\mathrm{ev}}=\bigoplus_{j\text{ even}}E^j$, $E^{\mathrm{odd}}=\bigoplus_{j\text{ odd}}E^j$, $d=\bigoplus_jd_j$, and $\Delta=(d+d^*)^2$. Assume that $\Delta$ has discrete nonnegative spectrum, smooth eigensections, a trace-class heat operator $e^{-t\Delta}$ with smooth kernel for every $t>0$, and a harmonic representative for each cohomology class. For $g\in T$, let $M^g=\{x\in M:g\cdot x=x\}$, and suppose each $x\in M^g$ is simple, meaning $I-dg_x:T_xM\to T_xM$ is invertible. If $U_g$ is the induced action on sections, then
+
+$$\lim_{t\downarrow0}\operatorname{Str}(U_g e^{-t\Delta})=\sum_{x\in M^g}\frac{\sum_{j=0}^N(-1)^j\operatorname{tr}(U_g|_{E_x^j})}{|\det_{\mathbb R}(I-dg_x)|}.$$
+
+Here $\operatorname{Str}$ is the trace on $E^{\mathrm{ev}}$ minus the trace on $E^{\mathrm{odd}}$. The simplicity condition makes $M^g$ a finite set; the determinant is the normal Jacobian at that isolated fixed point.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Zelin Yi, Theorem 1.1 and §§2–5: relative tangent-groupoid heat-trace limit](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2003.00955)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The manifold, compact torus action, equivariant first-order elliptic complex and Hodge heat data in the Statement, and an element $g\in T$ with simple fixed points.
+
+[F1] The section action is $U_gs(x)=\rho_g(s(g^{-1}\cdot x))$, and the differentials commute with it. [[def-equivariant-elliptic-complex-index]]
+
+[A1] The missing analytic input is the local relative-tangent-groupoid heat-kernel extension: after rescaling near the graph of $g$, the smooth heat-kernel family extends to $t=0$ with fiber kernel equal to that of the constant-coefficient operator determined by the principal symbol of $\Delta$. The source proves this in its groupoid construction, §§2–4, but no local carrier in this batch proves the parameter-dependent heat-kernel extension.
+
+## Proof
+
+**Proof technique:** Relative tangent-groupoid rescaling, conditional on [A1].
+
+**Given:** The Hodge heat operator and equivariant action in the Statement.
+
+1.1 On a local chart with coordinates $u$, the second-order Hodge Laplacian has the form $\Delta=-\sum_{|\alpha|=2}a_\alpha(u)\partial_u^\alpha+\sum_{|\beta|\le1}b_\beta(u)\partial_u^\beta$, with smooth matrix coefficients and positive elliptic principal symbol. Under the rescaling $u=p+tv$, its conjugate $t^2\Delta$ has principal part $-\sum_{|\alpha|=2}a_\alpha(p+tv)\partial_v^\alpha$ and lower-order terms multiplied by $t$ or $t^2$; thus its coefficients extend to the constant-symbol operator at $t=0$. The relative tangent groupoid uses these rescaled coordinates to join the pair-groupoid heat kernel at $t>0$ to that tangent operator at $t=0$. [given, F1, algebra]
+
+2.1 By [A1], the rescaled kernels extend smoothly across $t=0$. The trace integral is supported in neighborhoods of the fixed points in this limit: outside them the graph of $g$ and the diagonal have positive separation, so the heat-kernel Gaussian decay makes that contribution tend to zero. At a fixed point $x$, the tangent-fiber contribution is the integral of the constant-coefficient heat kernel evaluated at $(dg_x^{-1}-I)v$, with the fiber lift $U_g|_{E_x^\bullet}$ acting on its coefficients. [given, A1, step 1.1]
+
+2.2 The linear change of variables $w=(dg_x^{-1}-I)v$ contributes $|\det_{\mathbb R}(I-dg_x)|^{-1}$. The constant-coefficient heat kernel is the inverse Fourier transform of $\exp(-\sigma_2(\Delta)_x(\xi))$; integrating it over the tangent fiber evaluates that Fourier multiplier at $\xi=0$, which is the identity on $E_x^\bullet$. Taking its graded fiber trace gives $\sum_j(-1)^j\operatorname{tr}(U_g|_{E_x^j})$. Summing the finitely many fixed-point contributions proves the stated limit, conditional on the unresolved local input [A1]. [A1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
+
+## Proof references
+
+[Zelin Yi, §§2–5: relative tangent groupoid, rescaled heat kernels, and the Fourier-normalized fixed-point calculation](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2003.00955)
